From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Davi <davilimacarv2536@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 22:38:43 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] microtask owner context
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Carries the owning execution context from promise-reaction
*registration* to reaction *execution*. `await p` inside request A
registers a reaction; request B settles `p`; JSC then runs A's
continuation inside B's turn, so any ambient "who is running" state
names B for work that belongs to A (wrong log attribution, wrong
per-request CPU, wrong host-API identity). The reaction gets a
`uint64_t` owner token — opaque to JSC, not a JS value, so no write
barrier and the cell stays trivially destructible — snapshotted in
the `Status::Pending` arms of
`performPromiseThen`/`performPromiseThenWithInternalMicrotask` (the
settled arms enqueue in the same turn, where the running owner is
already correct). When `triggerPromiseReactions` sees a reaction whose
owner differs from the one running, it asks the embedder for a
dispatcher that restores that owner around the run — the same
wrapping seam `DebuggableMicrotaskDispatcher` already uses, so no
queue-layout or scheduling change — and enqueues it straight onto the
queue instead of through `queueMicrotask`. That bypass is the
load-bearing part: inside a restored continuation the ordinary enqueue
stamps the *ambient* owner, which would re-tag a reaction belonging to
the draining turn as the continuation that happens to be running. It
holds even when the dispatcher cannot be allocated, because falling
back there attaches a wrong owner rather than none. A separate field
rather than the reaction's existing `context` slot, because `await`
already stores the resumed async frame there; boxing both into one cell
is possible but costs an allocation per owned registration and forces
every reader of that slot to unbox. The engine also stores a promise's
first reaction inline, with no reaction cell to hold a token, so an
owned turn deliberately skips that fast path and spills to a real
reaction — otherwise the identity would be dropped on the most common
registration there is, and the same reasoning kills the DFG
classification that lowers `.then` into that inline write from
generated code. Embedder opts in via `colloSetOwnerHooks`; unset hooks
make every added operation a null check. Prerequisite for serving
concurrent requests in one worker, and the same mechanism
`AsyncLocalStorage` needs.
