+// Always declines. The inline reaction this classification enables has no slot for
+// the owner of the turn that registered it, and the fast path it lowers to writes
+// the reaction straight into the promise without ever calling performPromiseThen,
+// where the owner is captured -- so a `.then` that tiers up would lose the identity
+// of the request that registered it. Declining keeps the generic PerformPromiseThen
+// node, which calls operationPerformPromiseThen and lands in the guarded C++ path.
+//
+// An embedder that installs the owner hooks gives up nothing: an owned turn is
+// forbidden from taking the inline path in performPromiseThen, so the fast path
+// this used to enable could never have been taken in good faith. One that does
+// not install them does pay, trading the inline stores for a call into
+// operationPerformPromiseThen, whose C++ still takes the inline path. The two
+// call sites below already handle the empty answer, and
+// PerformPromiseThenOneHandler is then never created, which leaves no lowering
+// in either backend that could go stale.
